    "durationMs": 267945,
    "finalText": "The URL shortener is complete and fully verified. I built a NestJS 11 + TypeORM/PostgreSQL app (the starter had only dependencies, no skeleton) with these pieces:\n\n- **Persistence**: TypeORM with a `links` table (`code` unique, `url`, `clicks`, auto-increment `id`), applied via a proper migration (`npm run migration:run` in `start.sh`), connection string read from `DATABASE_URL` — nothing hard-coded.\n- **Endpoints**: `POST /links` (201 with generated or custom code, 409 on duplicate), `GET /links` (ascending creation order with `total`), `GET /links/{code}` (404 JSON), `GET /r/{code}` (302 + Location, atomic `clicks + 1` via SQL `RETURNING`, so increments are exactly-once even under concurrency).\n- **Validation**: malformed JSON, missing/invalid `url`, and invalid codes (4–20 of `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`) all return 422 `{\"error\": ...}` — handled by a custom JSON body-parser plus a global exception filter that renders every error as JSON.\n\nI tested the whole contract live: creation, duplicate 409, all 422 paths, 404s, redirect Location header, concurrent click increments, list ordering, and a full server restart confirming data and click counts survive. Then I stopped the server and truncated my test rows so the harness starts from a clean table — `sh start.sh` builds, migrates, and serves on `0.0.0.0:8000`."
  }
